L000353	\textit{Beyond adiabaticity.}---Previous works prepared bosonic code states using Hamiltonian Floquet engineering (HFE) combined with adiabatic ramping (AR)~\cite{LZGuo2025CP,gkp2024prl}.
L000354	In those protocols, periodic driving is used to mimic an effective Hamiltonian whose eigenstates encode the logical code states, and a slowly varying ramp then prepares the target state (i.e., an arbitrary superposition of the logical code states) from a suitably chosen initial state (with nonzero overlap with the target state) via adiabatic evolution.
L000355	However, it has been shown that AR protocols typically require evolution over \emph{thousands} of driving periods to achieve high fidelity~\cite{LZGuo2025CP,gkp2024prl}.
L000356	Even with machine-learning-assisted optimization~\cite{guo2025RL}, the protocol remains slow and is therefore vulnerable to decoherence and control noise on current hardware.
L000357	
L000358	{Our single-period Floquet (SF) approach goes beyond this paradigm by abandoning AR altogether.
L000359	Instead, it directly synthesizes a target unitary that prepares the desired state from an arbitrary initial state within a single Floquet period.
L000360	As a result, the SF method dramatically reduces the control time and exhibits substantially improved robustness under realistic noise conditions~\cite{SM}.
L000361	When further combined with optimal pulse engineering, it achieves even higher accuracy without sacrificing its short-duration advantage.}
L000362	
L000363	{In Table.~\ref{table:complexity}, we benchmark the performance of the SF and AR methods, as well as their optimized variants, in terms of timescale, state-preparation fidelity, and robustness under identical noisy environments. Here, robustness is defined as the maximum tolerable noise strength for achieving high-fidelity ($>0.99$) code-state preparation under the same noise model (see the supplemental material~\cite{SM} for detailed numerical setup and the full robustness analysis).
L000364	Overall, our SF method significantly outperforms the AR protocol, yielding improvements of up to three orders of magnitude across all considered metrics.
L000365	}
