L000538	\section{Appendix C:. Analytic Constructions in Truncated Fock Spaces: Principal-Branch Generators and Householder Unitary Synthesis}
L000539	
L000540	In this section, we present two core analytical derivations: (i) the construction of the principal-branch Hamiltonian that generates a given unitary $\hat{U}\in \mathbb{U}(N)$ on a truncated Fock space, and (ii) a closed-form synthesis of a unitary operator that maps a prescribed normalized initial state to a normalized final state, achieved via a Householder reflection combined with a rank-one phase correction.
L000541	
L000542	
L000543	\subsection{Appendix C.1: Principal-branch Hamiltonian from a given unitary}
L000544	\label{sec:principal-branch}
L000545	
L000546	Let $\hat{U}\in \mathbb{U}(N)$ be a unitary operator acting on the truncated Fock space
L000547	$\mathrm{span}\{\ket{0},\ldots,\ket{N-1}\}$ and assume
L000548	that $\hat{U}$ is implemented as the time evolution under a (priori unknown)
L000549	time–independent Hamiltonian $\hat{H}$ over a finite duration $T>0$, i.e.,
L000550	\begin{equation}
L000551	    \hat{U} = \exp\!\left(-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar} \hat{H}T\right),\qquad T>0 .
L000552	    \tag{B1}
L000553	    \label{B1}
L000554	\end{equation}
L000555	Our goal is to reconstruct a Hermitian generator
L000556	$\hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}$ such that: (i) it reproduces the given unitary $\hat{U}$, and
L000557	(ii) its eigenvalues lie on the principal branch of the complex logarithm.
L000558	We refer to such a generator $\hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}$ as the \emph{principal-branch
L000559	Hamiltonian} associated with $\hat{U}$.
L000560	
L000561	
L000562	By the spectral theory, there exists a unitary $\hat{V}$ and eigenvalues
L000563	$\lambda_k$ of $\hat{U}$ such that
L000564	\begin{equation}
L000565	    \hat{U} = \hat{V} \, \mathrm{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_N)\, \hat{V}^\dagger .
L000566	    \tag{B2}
L000567	\end{equation}
L000568	Since $\hat{U}$ is unitary, each eigenvalue can be written as $\lambda_k = \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\theta_k}$
L000569	with some real phases $\theta_k$. To obtain a unique logarithm, we choose the
L000570	\emph{principal arguments} $\theta_k\in(-\pi,\pi]$, i.e., we restrict
L000571	all eigenphases to this interval. This choice amounts to using the
L000572	principal branch of the complex logarithm, which resolves the ambiguity in the
L000573	generator up to integer multiples of $2\pi/T$.
L000574	
L000575	The principal matrix logarithm of $\hat{U}$ is then defined via the holomorphic functional calculus as
L000576	\begin{equation}
L000577	    \log \hat{U}
L000578	    \equiv \hat{V} \,\mathrm{diag}\bigl(\log\lambda_1,\ldots,\log\lambda_N\bigr)\, \hat{V}^\dagger
L000579	     = -\mathrm{i}\, \hat{V}\,\mathrm{diag}(\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_N)\,\hat{V}^\dagger .
L000580	    \tag{B3}
L000581	\end{equation}
L000582	Here $\log\lambda_k$ denotes the complex logarithm evaluated on the principal branch, i.e., $\log\lambda_k=-\mathrm{i}\theta_k$ with
L000583	$\theta_k\in(-\pi,\pi]$.
L000584	
L000585	Taking the principal logarithm of Eq.~(\ref{B1}) yields
L000586	\begin{equation}
L000587	    -\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar}\hat{H}T = \log \hat{U}
L000588	    \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
L000589	    \hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}} = \frac{\mathrm{i}\hbar}{T}\,\log \hat{U}
L000590	                    = \frac{\hbar}{T}\,
L000591	                      \hat{V}\,\mathrm{diag}(\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_N)\,\hat{V}^\dagger .
L000592	    \tag{B4}
L000593	    \label{B4}
L000594	\end{equation}
L000595	Thus, $\hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}$ is Hermitian and generates $\hat{U}$ over time $T$:
L000596	$\exp(-\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\hbar}\hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}T)=\hat{U}$, with all its eigenvalues $\frac{\hbar}{T}\theta_k$ lying within the interval
L000597	$(-\pi\hbar/T,\pi\hbar/T]$. In particular, $\hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}$ is the
L000598	\emph{unique} such generator, as long as none of the eigenvalues of $\hat{U}$ lies exactly at
L000599	$-1$ (corresponding to $\theta_k=\pm\pi$), which would place them on the branch
L000600	cut of the complex logarithm.
L000601	
L000602	Equivalently, let $\ket{u_k}$ be an orthonormal eigenbasis of $\hat{U}$, satisfying $\hat{U}\ket{u_k}=\lambda_k\ket{u_k}$, and define the rank-one spectral projectors
L000603	\begin{equation*}
L000604	    \hat{P}_k := \ket{u_k}\!\bra{u_k} .
L000605	\end{equation*}
L000606	Then Eq.~(\ref{B4}) can be expressed in the compact spectral form
L000607	\begin{equation}
L000608	    \hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}
L000609	      = \frac{\hbar}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\theta_k \hat{P}_k,
L000610	    \tag{B5}
L000611	\end{equation}
L000612	which explicitly shows that $\hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}$ acts by multiplying each
L000613	eigenvector $\ket{u_k}$ of $\hat{U}$ by the corresponding eigenvalue
L000614	$\frac{\hbar}{T}\theta_k$.
L000615	
L000616	In the Fock basis $\{\ket{n}\}_{n=0}^{N-1}$, the matrix elements of $\hat{H}_{\rm pr}$ are given by
L000617	\begin{equation}
L000618	    H_{mn}
L000619	      = \left[\frac{\hbar}{T}
L000620	          \hat{V}\,\mathrm{diag}(\theta_k)\,\hat{V}^\dagger
L000621	        \right]_{mn}
L000622	      = \frac{\hbar}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\theta_k
L000623	        \braket{m|u_k}\braket{u_k|n},
L000624	    \tag{B6}
L000625	    \label{B6}
L000626	\end{equation}
L000627	which provides a practical recipe for computing $\hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}$ from a
L000628	numerically known unitary $\hat{U}$: diagonalize $\hat{U}$, extract the principal
L000629	arguments of its eigenvalues, and construct $\hat{H}_{\mathrm{pr}}$ via
L000630	Eq.~(\ref{B6}). This procedure is employed in the main text to associate an
L000631	effective Hamiltonian with the synthesized unitary gates acting on the truncated space of a harmonic oscillator.
L000632	
L000633	
L000634	\subsection{Appendix C.2: Unitary synthesis via Householder transformation}
L000635	\label{sec:householder}
L000636	
L000637	In this section, we present an explicit construction of a unitary operator that
L000638	maps a given pure state $\ket{\psi_i}$ to another pure state
L000639	$\ket{\psi_f}$ within the same Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_N$. The
L000640	construction is based on the (quantum) Householder transformation~\cite{Urias2010}, which corresponds to a reflection across a hyperplane in $\mathcal{H}_N$.
L000641	Throughout this section, we assume that both states are normalized, i.e., $\braket{\psi_i|\psi_i} = \braket{\psi_f|\psi_f}=1$.
L000642	
L000643	We begin by introducing the complex overlap between the initial and final states:
L000644	\begin{equation}
L000645	r \equiv \braket{\psi_f | \psi_i} \in \mathbb{C},
L000646	\end{equation}
L000647	and define a phase factor $\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}$ such that
L000648	\begin{equation}
L000649	    \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi} =
L000650	    \begin{cases}
L000651	        \dfrac{r}{|r|}, & r \neq 0, \\
L000652	        1,                        & r = 0.
L000653	    \end{cases}
L000654	    \label{eq:phi_def}
L000655	
L000656	\end{equation}
L000657	It is clear that $\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\phi}r= |r|\ge 0$ is real and
L000658	nonnegative: geometrically, this operation removes only the global phase of the overlap and does not affect the relative orientation of the two states in Hilbert space.
L000659	
L000660	\paragraph{Generic case: non–collinear states.}
L000661	Suppose that $\ket{\psi_i}$ and $\ket{\psi_f}$ are not collinear, i.e.\ $|r| \neq 1$. We define the vector
L000662	\begin{equation}
L000663	    \ket{v} \equiv \ket{\psi_i} - \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}\ket{\psi_f},
L000664	    \qquad
L000665	    \ket{u} \equiv \frac{\ket{v}}{\|\ket{v}\|},
L000666	\end{equation}
L000667	and introduce the corresponding Householder reflector~\cite{Urias2010}
L000668	\begin{equation}
L000669	    \hat{H}_{h} \equiv \hat{I} - 2 \ket{u}\!\bra{u}.
L000670	    \label{eq:H_def}
L000671	
L000672	\end{equation}
L000673	By construction, $\hat{H}_{h}$ is Hermitian (i.e., $\hat{H}_{h}^\dagger = \hat{H}_{h}$) and satisfies
L000674	\begin{equation}
L000675	    \hat{H}_{h}^2 = \left(\hat{I} - 2\ket{u}\!\bra{u} \right)^2
L000676	        = \hat{I} - 4\ket{u}\!\bra{u} + 4\ket{u}\!\braket{u|u}\!\bra{u}
L000677	        = \hat{I},
L000678	\end{equation}
L000679	which shows that $\hat{H}_{h}$ is also unitary. Thus, $\hat{H}_{h}$ represents a reflection about the hyperplane orthogonal to $\ket{u}$.
L000680	
L000681	Next, we introduce a phase gate that acts nontrivially only on the target state:
L000682	\begin{equation}
L000683	    \hat{P}_{h} \equiv \hat{I} + \left(\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\phi}-1\right)\ket{\psi_f}\!\bra{\psi_f},
L000684	    \label{eq:P_def}
L000685	
L000686	\end{equation}
L000687	which multiplies $\ket{\psi_f}$ by $\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\phi}$ and
L000688	acts as the identity on any state orthogonal to $\ket{\psi_f}$.
L000689	
L000690	Finally, we construct the unitary operator
L000691	\begin{equation}
L000692	    \hat{U} \equiv \hat{P}_{h} \hat{H}_{h}.
L000693	    \label{eq:U_def}
L000694	
L000695	\end{equation}
L000696	Since both $\hat{P}_{h}$ and $\hat{H}_{h}$ are unitary, their product $\hat{U}$ is also unitary. We now verify explicitly that
L000697	$\hat{U}$ maps $\ket{\psi_i}$ to $\ket{\psi_f}$ as desired.
L000698	
L000699	\textit{Proof.}
L000700	Using the identity $\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\phi}r = |r|$, we first compute the norm of $\ket{v}$:
L000701	\begin{align}
L000702	    \| \ket{v} \|^2
L000703	      &= \braket{v|v}
L000704	       = \braket{\psi_i|\psi_i}
L000705	         - \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\phi}\braket{\psi_f|\psi_i}
L000706	         - \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}\braket{\psi_i|\psi_f}
L000707	         + \braket{\psi_f|\psi_f} \notag \\
L000708	      &= 2 - \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\phi}r
L000709	              - \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}r^*
L000710	       = 2\bigl(1-|r|\bigr).
L000711	    \label{eq:v_norm}
L000712	
L000713	\end{align}
L000714	We also have
L000715	\begin{equation}
L000716	    \braket{v|\psi_i}
L000717	      = 1 - \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\phi}r
L000718	      = 1 - |r|.
L000719	\end{equation}
L000720	For $|r|<1$, this quantity is nonzero, and one can evaluate the action of $\hat{H}_{h}$:
L000721	\begin{align}
L000722	    \hat{H}_{h}\ket{\psi_i}
L000723	      &= \ket{\psi_i}
L000724	         - 2\ket{v}\frac{\braket{v|\psi_i}}{\| \ket{v}\|^2} \notag \\
L000725	      &= \ket{\psi_i}
L000726	         - 2\ket{v}\,
L000727	           \frac{1-|\alpha|}{2(1-|\alpha|)}
L000728	       = \ket{\psi_i} - \ket{v}
L000729	       = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}\ket{\psi_f}.
L000730	    \label{eq:H_on_psi_i}
L000731	
L000732	\end{align}
L000733	Therefore, the Householder reflector $\hat{H}_{h}$ maps the initial state to the target state up to the controlled phase factor $\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}$.
L000734	
L000735	By construction of $\hat{P}_{h}$, we have
L000736	\begin{equation}
L000737	    \hat{P}_{h}\ket{\psi_f} = \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\phi}\ket{\psi_f},
L000738	\end{equation}
L000739	and $\hat{P}_{h}$ acts as the identity on any vector orthogonal to $\ket{\psi_f}$.
L000740	Combining this with Eq.~\eqref{eq:H_on_psi_i}, we finally obtain
L000741	\begin{equation}
L000742	    \hat{U}\ket{\psi_i}
L000743	      = \hat{P}_{h} \hat{H}_{h} \ket{\psi_i}
L000744	      = \hat{P}_{h}\bigl(\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}\ket{\psi_f}\bigr)
L000745	      = \ket{\psi_f}.
L000746	    \label{eq:U_maps}
L000747	
L000748	\end{equation}
L000749	Hence, $\hat{U}$ is a desired unitary operator that exactly transforms the initial
L000750	state $\ket{\psi_{i}}$ into the target state $\ket{\psi_{f}}$ whenever $|\alpha|\neq 1$.
L000751	
L000752	\paragraph{Collinear case.}
L000753	If the two states differ only by a global phase, i.e., $\ket{\psi_f} = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\theta}\ket{\psi_i}$ with
L000754	$\theta\in\mathbb{R}$, the above construction becomes singular since
L000755	$\ket{v}=0$. In this special case, we can directly define the unitary operator as
L000756	\begin{equation}
L000757	    \hat{U} = \hat{I} + (\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\theta}-1)\ket{\psi_i}\!\bra{\psi_i},
L000758	    \label{eq:collinear-U}
L000759	
L000760	\end{equation}
L000761	which is simply a one-dimensional phase operation.  It is straightforward to verify that $\hat{U}$ is unitary and satisfies
L000762	$\hat{U} \ket{\psi_i} = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\theta}\ket{\psi_i}
L000763	 = \ket{\psi_f}$.
L000764	
L000765	\medskip
L000766	To summarize, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:phi_def}--\eqref{eq:collinear-U} provide an
L000767	explicit and constructive method for synthesizing a unitary operator that maps an arbitrary
L000768	normalized initial state $\ket{\psi_i}$ to a normalized target state $\ket{\psi_f}$.
L000769	
L000770	
L000771	\\
L000772	
L000773	{Note that this construction strategy is not unique. For two normalized states $|\psi_i\rangle$ and $|\psi_f\rangle$, there exist infinitely many unitary operators $\hat{U}$ satisfying $\hat{U}|\psi_i\rangle = |\psi_f\rangle$. In principle, one may adopt different approaches, including Householder reflections~\cite{Urias2010}, basis-completion methods~\cite{Edelman1998}, or numerical optimization on constrained matrix manifolds~\cite{Absil2008}.
L000774	In the present work, we adopt the Householder construction because it provides a closed-form and non-iterative solution that is unitary by construction and maps the initial state to the target state exactly, up to machine precision in numerical implementation. To illustrate its numerical performance, we show in Fig.~\ref{fig:figure_Householder} the infidelity of the Householder-based unitary construction as a function of the Hilbert-space dimension, where the state fidelity is defined as ($F =|\langle\psi_f|U|\psi_i\rangle|^2$). For randomly chosen initial and target states, the mapping infidelity remains at the level of $10^{-15}$ even up to dimension $d=2^{13}$. Each error bar denotes the standard deviation over $10^2$ numerical trials, and the full simulation takes less than 10 seconds on a standard laptop. A key practical advantage of the Householder method is that it depends only on the two states of interest and does not require explicitly constructing the remaining orthogonal subspace. Moreover, unlike iterative numerical approaches, it avoids initialization dependence, convergence issues, and residual optimization errors.}
L000775	
