L001010	\section{Appendix H: Hardware-aware noise}
L001011	
L001012	
L001013	To investigate the impact of a noisy environment, we model the system dynamics using the Lindblad master equation in the rotating frame:
L001014	\beq
L001015	\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\hat{\rho}(t)=-\frac{i}{\lambda}\left[\hat{H}(t), \hat{\rho}(t)\right]+\kappa\mathcal{D}[\hat{a}]\hat{\rho}(t),
L001016	\eeq
L001017	where $\hat{\rho}(t)$ is the density operator of the system and $\hat{a}$ is the bosonic annihilation operator. The second term on the right-hand side describes the energy relaxation of the cavity due to single-photon loss at rate $\kappa$, with the Lindblad dissipator defined as $\mathcal{D}[\hat{O}]\hat{\rho}=\hat{O}\hat{\rho}\hat{O}^{\dag}-(\hat{O}^{\dag}\hat{O}\hat{\rho}+\hat{\rho}\hat{O}^{\dag}\hat{O})/2$.
L001018	
L001019	\textit{Incoherent error.} As described above, the dominant decoherence mechanism in many bosonic systems, such as superconducting cavities and trapped-ion oscillators, is energy relaxation through single-photon loss.
L001020	For a gate of duration $T$, one can estimate the fundamental fidelity limit imposed by this loss channel in the absence of quantum jumps (i.e., in the no-jump trajectory). Let $\bar{ n }(t)
L001021	= \langle \hat{a}^\dagger \hat{a}\rangle(t)$ denote the instantaneous mean photon
L001022	number during the unitary (lossless) evolution. Then the probability that no
L001023	photon is lost throughout the gate duration is approximately given by
L001024	\begin{equation}\label{eq:coherent_limit}
L001025	  \mathcal{F}_{\rm gate}(T)\;\lesssim\;
L001026	  \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{coh}}^{\max}(T)=
L001027	  \exp\!\left[
L001028	    -\kappa \int_0^T \bar{ n }(t)\, dt
L001029	  \right].
L001030	\end{equation}
L001031	This expression provides a simple yet informative upper bound on the achievable gate
L001032	fidelity, set solely by the incoherent energy relaxation (i.e., the single-photon loss channel).
L001033	
L001034	{In Fig.~\ref{fig:incoherent_noise}, we show the infidelity of bosonic code-state preparation as a function of the (normalized) single-photon loss rate $\kappa/\omega_{0}$, varied over the range $\kappa/\omega_0 \in [10^{-6}, 10^{-1}]$.
L001035	For the bare QLG protocol (blue triangles), the infidelity saturates at the level of $\sim 10^{-2}$ in the limit $\kappa \to 0$, indicating that its performance is predominantly limited by coherent errors such as the rotating-wave approximation and Trotterization.
L001036	In contrast, owing to the short total evolution time of our protocol compared to the cavity coherence time, the optimized QLG scheme (opt-QLG, red circles) achieves significantly higher fidelities and closely follows the coherent upper bound given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:coherent_limit}), shown as solid black lines.}
