L001048	\textit{Coherent error.}
L001049	In our setup, coherent errors refer to control inaccuracies induced by imperfections in experimental instrumentation, such as amplitude and frequency deviations. These inaccuracies typically arise in superconducting circuits due to flux noise or distortions in waveform generators.
L001050	
L001051	For a single Josephson junction (JJ) embedded in a SQUID loop with tunable amplitude and phase, the effective driving potential takes the form
L001052	\beq\label{eq-squid}
L001053	V_{\mathrm{SQUID}}(\hat{\varphi})=-E_J\cos\left(\frac{\Phi_{sq}}{\Phi_0}\pi\right)\cos\left[\frac{M(\Phi_x)}{L}\hat{\varphi}+\frac{\Phi_{ext}}{\Phi_0}\right],
L001054	\eeq
L001055	where $\Phi_0$ is the magnetic flux quantum and $M(\Phi_x)/L$ describes the flux-to-phase conversion.
L001056	We consider a harmonically modulated external flux (in units of $\Phi_0$) of the form
L001057	\beq
L001058	\Phi(t) = \Phi_\text{dc} + f(t)\cos\big(\omega_\Phi t + \phi_0\big),
L001059	\eeq
L001060	where $\Phi_{\rm dc}$ is a static offset, $f(t)$ is a slowly varying modulation envelope, and $\omega_\Phi$ is the carrier frequency.
L001061	Note that in realistic experiments, both the DC and AC components are subject to fluctuations. In view of this, we model the noisy external flux as
L001062	\beq
L001063	\Phi'(t) = \Phi_{\rm dc}+\epsilon_{\rm dc}(t)+ f(t)\cos\big[(\omega_\Phi+\epsilon_{\rm ac}(t)) t + \phi_0\big],
L001064	\eeq
L001065	where $\epsilon_{\rm dc}(t)$ and $\epsilon_{\rm ac}(t)$ represent the time-dependent amplitude and frequency fluctuations, respectively. {These perturbations result in coherent errors of the effective driving potential, which is expressed as
L001066	\beqa
L001067	\hat{V}'(\hat{x},t)=\int_{-\infty}^{+\infty} \beta(t)A'(k, t)\cos[k\hat{x}+\phi'(k,t)]dk.
L001068	\eeqa
L001069	}
L001070	
L001071	{
L001072	To simulate the effect of coherent noise numerically, we incorporate fluctuations in both the control envelope and the drive frequency by making the replacements
L001073	\beq
L001074	\beta(t) \rightarrow \beta(t)+\epsilon_{\rm dc}(t), \quad \omega_\Phi \rightarrow \omega_\Phi+\epsilon_{\rm ac}(t),
L001075	\eeq
L001076	where the amplitude noise $\epsilon_{\rm dc}(t)$ and frequency noise $\epsilon_{\rm ac}(t)$ are modeled as Gaussian white-noise processes, i.e.,
L001077	 \beq
L001078	 \epsilon_{\rm dc/ac}(t) \equiv \zeta_{\rm dc/ac}\xi(t).
L001079	\eeq
L001080	Here, $\zeta_{\rm dc/ac}$ sets the corresponding noise strength, and $\xi(t)$ denotes standard Gaussian white noise with $\langle\xi(t)\rangle=0$ and $\langle\xi(t)\xi(t')\rangle=\delta(t-t')$.
