L001147	{
L001148	\section{Appendix I:. Controllability Benchmark}
L001149	\textit{\textbf{SNAP-based circuit}}.---To realize universal gate operation on a logical qubit encoded in a quantum oscillator, one typically combines displacement operations with selective number-dependent arbitrary phase (SNAP) gates in a resonator--qubit architecture~\cite{Liang2015praR,heeres2017snap}.
L001150	Since displacement and SNAP operations act on the resonator and the ancillary qubit, respectively~\cite{Liang2015praR}, they are applied sequentially rather than simultaneously.
L001151	This composite operation has become a standard building block for realizing arbitrary unitaries on CV logical qubits, and full $U(d)$ control has been experimentally demonstrated in circuit-QED platforms~\cite{heeres2017snap}.
L001152	For simplicity, the composite operation can be written as
L001153	\beq
L001154	\hat{\mathcal{D}}(\alpha)\hat{S}_n(\vartheta) = e^{i\alpha(\hat{a}^\dagger - \hat{a})} e^{-i \vartheta_n |n\rangle\langle n|},
L001155	\eeq
L001156	where $\hat{\mathcal{D}}(\alpha)$ is the displacement operator and $\hat{S}_n(\vartheta)$ denotes a SNAP gate with number-dependent phases $\{\vartheta_n\}$.
L001157	Since the universality proof is formulated in the infinitesimal-displacement limit, controllability does not rely on any nonzero lower bound on $|\alpha|$. In practice, however, $|\alpha|$ should be chosen small enough to maintain the validity of a truncated Fock-space description~\cite{Liang2015praR}.
L001158	In the numerics below, we therefore take $\alpha$ in the perturbative regime and use $|\alpha|\sqrt{d}\ll 1$ as a practical guideline to suppress leakage outside the truncated subspace.}
L001159	
L001160	{We consider a SNAP+displacement circuit ensemble acting on the truncated Fock space $\mathcal H_d=\mathrm{span}\{|0\rangle,\dots,|d-1\rangle\}$. Starting from $|0\rangle$, each sample is generated by an $L$-layer circuit
L001161	\begin{equation}
L001162	\hat{\mathcal{C}}_{\rm snap}=\prod_{\ell=1}^{L} \prod_{j=1}^{d}\hat{D}(\alpha_{\ell}) \hat{S}(\theta_{j,\ell} ),
L001163	\end{equation}
L001164	where $\alpha\in\mathbb{C}$ is a complex displacement amplitude and $\vartheta_{j,\ell}$ denotes the photon-number-selective phase applied to $|j\rangle$ in layer $\ell$. Each displacement contributes two real trainable parameters (its real and imaginary parts), so the circuit contains
L001165	$L\times (d+2)$ real trainable parameters in total. To generate ensembles with an arbitrary number of trainable parameters, we include all parameters from the first several complete SNAP layers, while any remaining parameters are randomly sampled from the subsequent layer.
L001166	The displacement amplitudes are sampled uniformly from the disk $|\alpha_{\ell}|\le 1/\sqrt{d}$, ensuring operation in the perturbative regime and suppressing leakage outside $\mathcal H_d$.}
L001167	
L001168	{\textit{\textbf{QLG-based circuit}}.---For the QLG ansatz, the gate parameters are obtained analytically. We therefore construct a parametrized circuit by discretizing the control envelope into piecewise-constant values $\beta(t_n)$, yielding
L001169	\begin{equation}
L001170	\hat{\mathcal{C}}_{\rm qlg} = \prod_{n=1}^{N_t}{\hat{\mathbf{G}}}[\vec{\beta}(t_n)].
L001171	\label{eq:Trotter_error}
L001172	\end{equation}
L001173	where $N_t$ denotes the number of time-discretization steps (i.e., the Trotter depth). For a given target state, all other QLG parameters are determined uniquely by the analytic construction. Consequently, the only trainable degrees of freedom of the QLG circuit are the discrete control values, which we collect into the vector $\vec{\beta}(t_n)=[\beta_1,\beta_2,...,\beta_{N_t}]$.
L001174	}
